---
import { EXTENSION_META } from '@data/extension-meta';
import LegalLayout from '@layouts/legal-layout.astro';

const issuesUrl = `${EXTENSION_META.repositoryUrl}/issues`;
const licenseUrl = `${EXTENSION_META.repositoryUrl}/blob/HEAD/LICENSE.md`;
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Terms of Service — HeaderTweaker"
  description="The terms for using the free, open-source HeaderTweaker browser extension and the headertweaker.com website."
  heading="Terms of Service"
  lastUpdated={new Date('2026-11-07')}
>
  <p>
    These terms apply to your use of the {EXTENSION_META.name} browser extension for Firefox and
    Chrome ("the extension") and the website headertweaker.com ("the website"). By installing or
    using the extension, or using the website, you agree to these terms. If you don't agree, please
    don't use them.
  </p>

  <h2>The software and its license</h2>
  <p>
    {EXTENSION_META.name}
    is free, open-source software maintained by the developers of the HeaderTweaker repository. Its
    source code is released under the <a href={licenseUrl}>MIT License</a>, which lets you use,
    copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense and sell copies of the software, as long as
    the copyright and license notice are included. These terms don't restrict any rights the MIT
    License gives you. If the two ever conflict, the MIT License wins for the source code.
  </p>

  <h2>Price</h2>
  <p>
    The extension is free. There are no paid tiers, subscriptions, in-app purchases or
    advertisements.
  </p>

  <h2>Your responsibilities</h2>
  <p>
    HeaderTweaker sends the headers you configure to the websites your rules target. You are
    responsible for the rules you create and for how you use them. In particular, you agree:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      to use the extension only in line with applicable laws, and with the terms of the websites and
      services you send requests to;
    </li>
    <li>
      not to use it to gain unauthorized access to systems, accounts or data, to impersonate other
      people, or to get around security measures you're not permitted to bypass;
    </li>
    <li>
      to keep sensitive header values, such as API keys and tokens, safe — including in any files
      you export.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your data</h2>
  <p>
    The extension stores your rules and settings only in your browser and does not send them to us.
    See the <a href="/privacy-policy">Privacy Policy</a> for details.
  </p>

  <h2>No warranty</h2>
  <p>
    As stated in the MIT License, the software is provided
    <strong>"as is", without warranty of any kind</strong>, express or implied, including but not
    limited to the warranties of merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose and
    non-infringement. Modified headers can change how websites behave; it's up to you to check that
    the results are what you expect.
  </p>

  <h2>Limitation of liability</h2>
  <p>
    To the fullest extent permitted by law, the authors and copyright holders are not liable for any
    claim, damages or other liability, whether in an action of contract, tort or otherwise, arising
    from, out of or in connection with the software, the website, or their use. Nothing in these
    terms excludes liability that can't be excluded under the law that applies to you.
  </p>

  <h2>Updates and availability</h2>
  <p>
    The extension may be updated, changed or discontinued at any time, and its features may change
    between versions. Updates are delivered through the Firefox Add-ons and Chrome Web Store
    listings, whose own terms also apply to downloads from those stores.
  </p>

  <h2>Website content</h2>
  <p>
    The website is provided for information only. The {EXTENSION_META.name} name and logo identify
    the project; Firefox, Chrome and GitHub are trademarks of their respective owners. Links to
    third-party sites are provided for convenience, and we are not responsible for their content.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to these terms</h2>
  <p>
    These terms may be updated from time to time. The "Last updated" date above shows when they last
    changed, and their history is public in the
    <a href={EXTENSION_META.repositoryUrl}>source repository</a>. Continuing to use the extension or
    website after a change means you accept the updated terms.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about these terms? Open an issue on
    <a href={issuesUrl}>GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
